 journeyman have been  wandering around the country since the  12th century to learn new methods and  gather the experience they need to  eventually become master artisans  nowadays however there are only around  500 journeyman on the road at any given  time in Germany around 10% of them women  people from a wide variety of crafts  take the trip including bookbinders the  Walkabout is also a tradition in Austria  Switzerland France and Denmark after so  much information I want to get an idea  of what this feels like so I offer to  work for meal and accommodation for the  night  I'm on a walkabout and would like to  work for food and lodging well we don't  have any work okay nothing to be done  then thanks bye like to offer labour in  exchange for food and shelter have you  gotten anything for me this banana and  five euros and I don't have to do  anything for it now I'm giving them to  you yes I have lots to do at home I'm  glad to give you something to do in the  gardens do you have anything to eat or  drink with me yes with you a package of  spaghetti two thirds of the Germans I  asked we're willing to give food money  or lodging I think for my next series  I'll go into vaults myself  glimpses into a world I've always been  curious about the tradition is no longer  as common as it once was but it hasn't  died out yet  time to turn my back on Wolfsburg and  wander further south the pressures  mounting and completing my own role  about in 80 days is beginning to look  like an almost insurmountable challenge
 

